        },
        {
          "abilityId": 17,
          "accessMode": "rw",
          "code": "alarm_set_1",
          "description": "1. Alarm threshold enable control and settings 1\n2. HEX, big-endian. Total alarm count * 4 bytes = total setting length.\n3. Message format\nEach alarm consists of 4 bytes: byte 1 (alarm exists); byte 2 (breaker control mode when the alarm occurs: 0x01 trip, 0x00 no action, alarm only); bytes 3-4: alarm threshold.\nExample: 0x01 01 00 00 03 01 00 14 means the breaker supports short-circuit alarm; when it occurs, it trips automatically and threshold is padded with 0. Supports overload alarm; when it occurs, it trips and threshold is 20kW. No other alarm functions. Note: Do not report bytes for alarms that do not exist.\n4. Communication logic\nA. After network pairing succeeds, it proactively reports to the cloud in the above format so the panel knows which alarms the breaker currently supports. If setting fails, re-pair and report again.\nB. Also reported once when the breaker powers on.\nC. The panel sends alarm settings according to the protocol.\n5. The app panel supports the first-byte capability list: 01 short-circuit alarm, 02 surge alarm, 03 overload alarm, 04 leakage alarm, 05 high-temperature alarm, 06 arcing alarm, 07 high power alarm, 08 leakage self-test abnormal alarm",
          "extensions": {
            "iconName": "icon-dp_warming",
            "attribute": "1024"
          },
          "name": "Alarm Setting 1",
          "typeSpec": {
            "type": "raw",
            "maxlen": 128
          }
        },
        {
          "abilityId": 18,
          "accessMode": "rw",
          "code": "alarm_set_2",
          "description": "1. Alarm threshold enable control and settings 2\n2. HEX, big-endian\n3. Message format\nEach alarm consists of 4 bytes: byte 1 (alarm exists); byte 2 (breaker control mode when the alarm occurs: 0x01 trip, 0x00 no action, alarm only); bytes 3-4: alarm threshold.\nExample: 0x01 01 00 3C 03 01 00 F5 means the breaker supports overcurrent alarm; when it occurs, it trips and threshold is 60A. Supports overvoltage alarm; when it occurs, it trips and threshold is 245V.\n4. Communication logic\nA. After network pairing succeeds, it proactively reports to the cloud in the above format so the panel knows which alarms the breaker currently supports. If setting fails, re-pair and report again.\nB. Also reported once when the breaker powers on.\nC. The panel sends alarm settings according to the protocol.",
          "extensions": {
            "iconName": "icon-dp_warming",
            "attribute": "1152"
          },
          "name": "Alarm Setting 2",
          "typeSpec": {
            "type": "raw",
            "maxlen": 128
          }
        },
        {
          "abilityId": 19,
          "accessMode": "ro",
          "code": "breaker_id",
          "description": "1. Device number\n2. Big-endian, string type\n3. Unit/precision\n4. Message format\n5. Communication logic\nA. Report 3 times during pairing to ensure successful reporting. If pairing succeeds, the panel displays the device number.\nB. If pairing succeeds but the panel does not show the device number, remove the device and configure again.\nC. Report once each time the breaker powers on.",
          "extensions": {
            "iconName": "icon-dp_mode",
            "attribute": "1152"
          },
          "name": "Device ID Display",
          "typeSpec": {
            "type": "string",
            "maxlen": 255
